united kingdom has given approval to a  trial that will expose  healthy volunteers to covert 19.  infection is guaranteed with a so-called  challenge trial challenge trial  challenge trial  as the pandemic progressed over the last  year you may have heard this term  challenge trial come up the idea was  that by deliberately exposing people to  the virus you could help speed up the  development of a vaccine  and it turns out a lot of people were up  for this you know everybody feels  terrible right now  here's something that may help the world  be a little bit better  so i was like great that sounds good to  me um let me sign up right now  ultimately challenge trials did not end  up being used to develop the vaccines  that are now in circulation  but now researchers in the uk are  turning to the challenge method saying  it will allow them to test the long-term  effectiveness of the vaccines  and learn more about the disease overall  it's restarted a controversy in the  scientific community over the safety  efficacy and morality of the practice i  spoke to some very smart people on both  sides of the argument  in an attempt to answer a question with  big implications for humanity's future  okay so we're here to talk about human  challenge trials but i guess first let's  talk about typical vaccine trials how do  typical vaccine trials work yeah so once  a vaccine candidate has been studied in  animals and in the laboratory it moves  into human  studies the idea is that once you're  vaccinated with either  a placebo of the actual candidate you're  exposed in  society to the pathogen and we want to  see that most of the infections  and disease occurred in people with the  placebo to determine that the vaccine is  actually protective and efficacious  so with a human challenge study we  deliberately  infect humans human volunteers  and look at essentially what happens to  them  you say i've got the viral agent covid  whatever it is  and i'm going to give it to people this  is art kaplan he's a researcher at nyu  who's pretty firmly in the pro  challenge trial column well historically  vaccines are tested  in large trials usually you're talking  30 40 50  60 000 or more subjects you could spend  six seven eight years trying to get data  the challenge study  basically is a way to speed up the trial  process  you may be able to get answers as  quickly as exposing 500  600 people maybe over a period of six  months to a year  so are human challenge trials new or  have they been done before  human challenge trials are not new um  scientists have been doing them for  quite a long time decades looking at  various diseases  malaria cholera influenza  are some of them walter reed did it as a  matter of fact with his malaria  experiments in cuba they deliberately  let mosquitoes bite them to get malaria  to test  antidotes assuming they again they want  to kill you although people have died in  challenge studies  we have a tradition in space medicine of  saying  gee i wonder how long people can survive  extreme  gravitational forces let's put them in a  centrifuge until they pass out  so there are many areas of medicine  healthcare  even public health where we do expose  people to risk  deliberately in order to learn  [Music]  this is the infamous trolley problem in  bioethics which is  should you sacrifice one person to say  five five people this is liza dawson the  chief of bioethics at the walter reed  army institute of research  she's not really opposed to challenge  trials but where covet is concerned  she's basically in the ante column  one of the issues is that we have less  ability to predict what will happen to  people  as a consequence of covet infection  something like 10 to 20 percent or  depending on the numbers  have long-term consequences of covet  infection that's not to be sneezed at  and people of all ages seem to be um  susceptible  to longer term consequences of covid we  are rolling the dice  that something terrible might happen or  something you know  fairly bad might happen to people in  these trials well i do understand the  critics  and some of their points of view you  know it's never easy to defend  deliberately giving someone  something that makes them sick that  violates sort of a core  norm of medical ethics do no harm but  i'm not really  buying into the uh it causes more harm  to do a challenge study it feels  psychologically different to give  somebody a disease  as opposed to saying gee i don't know  how you got it maybe it was on the  subway or maybe you should have worn a  mask or  maybe a new variant appeared we may feel  you know psychologically we didn't do it  nature did it  nonetheless at the cemetery they don't  care  you're relying on nature to infect you  but it's not like people aren't exposed  i'm not sure that that's significantly  morally better  than just saying i'll give you the  disease and we'll get a faster answer  and there are a lot of volunteers who  feel the same way the organization one  day sooner which advocates for people's  right to participate in challenge trials  says it's had almost forty 000 people  contact them offering to take part if  such a trial were to be conducted  two of those volunteers are lena  jeweller a recent graduate of johns  hopkins  and gabrielle kleinwacks a phd student  in colorado  safe to say they're both in the pro  column  so speaking personally why would you be  willing to be part of a challenge trial  i think you know initially i was looking  at this kind of  as a person who does human subjects  research and a person who  likes to participate in human challenge  research so one of the cool things about  being a student at hopkins  is that you have access to participate  in this like really cool research  and i've always found that fascinating  um you know just as a person to  participate to say okay i'm contributing  to this science that  may you know help someone else down the  road and then the challenge aspect of it  i was really swayed by this like  argument of like we can like this is so  much more efficient this is so much more  effective  i would say the very first place my mind  went  was so i'm jewish um and in our  philosophical traditions it said that to  save a single life  it's as if you've saved an entire world  in the argument for child trials the  original argument back in march  it was estimated that developing a  vaccine every day sooner that you  develop a vaccine  saves 10 000 lives  and i thought that that's amazing i  would do whatever it took to develop a  vaccine  one day sooner which is why why the  organization is called that  we all know what it means to donate your  time we all know what it means to donate  your money  but this was an opportunity i felt to to  donate health to try to share some of my  health  with others so there are reasonable  arguments on either side of the ethics  debate and a lot of people are willing  to take the risk  but some offer a different kind of  criticism of challenge trials  that they wouldn't actually accomplish  their goal of speeding up vaccine  development  that's the big beef put forward by  charles vare a canadian bioethicist  he says the current uk study is the  perfect example of just how long  challenge trials can take what they're  doing so they've  spent a number of months developing the  challenge strain  so making sure they've got a stable  strain where they haven't  introduced mutations through the  manufacturing  process the second step is what they're  just embarking on now  it's actually figuring out the protocol  for how they're  going to administer this virus and so  we're just beginning that process  now with incremental steps of increasing  dose of virus  each of those steps is going to take at  least a month  and so we're many months away  from them being at at the point where  they can even  test test vaccine advocates of challenge  studies by  and large haven't talked about all this  preparatory work that needs to be done  even from the first article that was  published about this they sort of talked  as if you know sort of next week we  could just start doing this right you  just  pump a room full of cover 19 and then  yeah everybody gets right  and of course these studies are are much  more  complicated uh than that and then  there's the fact that we got the current  slate of covet 19 vaccines in record  time  without using challenge trials the  reason  why we got a vaccine so quickly  was because of a decade worth of work  on related coronaviruses that caused  sores  and that caused mers and i think we had  the genome of sars kov2 the the novel  coronavirus published  on january 10th i think of 2020.  three days later we had our first  candidate vaccine 63 days later that  vaccine was tested for the first time in  human beings  by september of last year we had  six large phase three trials planning to  involve  280 000 people the the monumental size  of the achievement here  it would be difficult to overstate well  that's traditional vaccine development  with new technology and it just  highlights my point  challenge studies are just are just the  wrong  technology for a rapidly moving  pandemic  but some still see challenge trials  playing a crucial role in the pandemic  ironically precisely because we now have  working vaccines  as soon as things start to look very  promising we basically are in a  situation where those people who are in  a large scale trial let's just use  covin are going to start to say wait a  minute i don't want to be in the placebo  group anymore  i want to drop out and you can't finish  the study ethically you've got to let  those people know  you're in the placebo group you can be  pretty certain that nearly everybody  will drop out because  they're not going to hang around in the  placebo group are very likely  so the studies that we have undergoing  that are underway now  of covid vaccines are falling apart  because people are dropping out of them  left and right  and so we're not going to get answers  that we might want the things like how  long does this vaccine  last in terms of protection or do we  know whether it's protecting us against  new variants of the  virus that appear some also see  challenge trials as a path to developing  new vaccines that improve on the  existing ones  obviously at this point we've got  approved vaccines in the us and and  maybe eight or nine total globally  but there's still a lot of benefit to  these sorts of trials  we can expand the total pool of how many  different types of vaccines we have  available and that's important because a  lot of the vaccine candidates we have  now like the mrna vaccines they're hard  to transport  they take two doses all these things  that if we  continue to try new vaccines we can find  better vaccines that are easier to  distribute and that has a lot of equity  implication we can get those to rural  areas different countries with less  infrastructure  so so that's kind of like the big draw  here  predicting the future is more of an art  than a science especially when it comes  to global crises  almost no one would have guessed that  we'd have several working vaccines for a  coronavirus within a year  so it's impossible to say exactly what  role challenge trials will play moving  forward  but if there's anything that we've  learned from the pandemic it's that the  question of which path is the more  ethical one  often doesn't have an obvious answer the  other thing about challenge trials i'll  save because i don't want to  dismiss them i'm not trying to dismiss  their importance is  if we get to a safer way to do challenge  trials  then i think then i'm all in favor of  using them  once they're safer communities broadly  would need to understand what that's  about and  and basically endorse it which is part  of the recommendations from the who and  others  but if if they're safer such that we're  not asking people to take those risks  i think they could be really important  tool in the toolbox and  just as we do them now in malaria trials  they're a tool in the toolbox but  they're not the whole story  you

